1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,310
Senior Angular interview preparation, lesson 5.

2
00:00:03,310 --> 00:00:07,172
Versioning Angular Libraries: Compatibility and Safe Migration.

3
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:12,496
Define the public contract before choosing a version

4
00:00:13,621 --> 00:00:15,568
Welcome to the versioning lesson.

5
00:00:15,568 --> 00:00:19,463
We will classify twelve proposed changes, review an input deprecation,

6
00:00:19,463 --> 00:00:23,357
and repair a package that leaks internals and duplicates Angular.

7
00:00:23,357 --> 00:00:28,810
Semantic versioning becomes useful only when the package defines what its public contract includes.

8
00:00:28,810 --> 00:00:32,705
For a component library, that contract extends beyond method signatures.

9
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:37,772
Define the public contract before choosing a version · 2

10
00:00:38,898 --> 00:00:43,112
Inputs, outputs, selectors, tokens, documented DOM hooks, keyboard behavior, forms integration,

11
00:00:43,112 --> 00:00:46,944
defaults, peer ranges, and testing harnesses can all affect consumers.

12
00:00:46,944 --> 00:00:50,392
An accidental export can become an external dependency even

13
00:00:50,392 --> 00:00:53,457
if its author intended it to be private.

14
00:00:53,457 --> 00:00:56,522
A private intention does not remove a name

15
00:00:56,522 --> 00:00:59,205
from the package that was actually published.

16
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:03,824
Define the public contract before choosing a version · 3

17
00:01:04,949 --> 00:01:09,490
Ask whether a supported consumer that worked before can continue to work without modification.

18
00:01:09,490 --> 00:01:12,086
Then distinguish an intentional public promise from an

19
00:01:12,086 --> 00:01:14,357
unsupported dependency and consider the practical rollout.

20
00:01:14,357 --> 00:01:18,250
A change can be formally compatible and still deserve a visual review.

21
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:21,818
A change can fix a bug and still break a workaround.

22
00:01:21,818 --> 00:01:24,414
State the compatibility policy instead of assuming that

23
00:01:24,414 --> 00:01:26,685
the word fix determines the release number.

24
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:29,924
Input acceptance and output obligations

25
00:01:31,049 --> 00:01:33,286
An input accepts values from consumers.

26
00:01:33,286 --> 00:01:37,760
Widening its accepted type usually permits more existing uses without invalidating them.

27
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:41,116
Narrowing it can make an existing template stop compiling.

28
00:01:41,116 --> 00:01:46,336
A new required input forces consumers to provide information they did not previously need.

29
00:01:46,336 --> 00:01:50,065
An optional input with a harmless default is generally additive.

30
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:53,795
Input acceptance and output obligations · 2

31
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:56,265
An output is different.

32
00:01:56,265 --> 00:01:58,620
Consumers handle the values the library emits.

33
00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:01,311
Widening an output to include null can break

34
00:02:01,311 --> 00:02:04,002
a handler that dereferences the original non-null value.

35
00:02:04,002 --> 00:02:07,030
The library has increased the obligations of the receiver.

36
00:02:07,030 --> 00:02:10,057
Do not classify input and output changes with the

37
00:02:10,057 --> 00:02:13,085
same rule merely because both use a union type.

38
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:16,997
Input acceptance and output obligations · 3

39
00:02:18,123 --> 00:02:19,573
Defaults also carry meaning.

40
00:02:19,573 --> 00:02:24,648
Changing medium to small can alter layout in every template that omitted the size.

41
00:02:24,648 --> 00:02:29,724
Even if no compile error appears, consumers may rely on the old visual result.

42
00:02:29,724 --> 00:02:32,261
Evaluate documented guarantees, snapshots, and product impact.

43
00:02:32,261 --> 00:02:36,974
A complete interview answer says which changes are clearly breaking and which depend

44
00:02:36,974 --> 00:02:41,325
on the contract, then explains the evidence that resolves each uncertain case.

45
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:44,857
Behavior and presentation can break consumers

46
00:02:45,983 --> 00:02:50,144
Switching to OnPush can expose consumers that mutate an object in

47
00:02:50,144 --> 00:02:54,305
place or dynamically insert a child that relies on zone-triggered checks.

48
00:02:54,305 --> 00:02:58,845
A keyboard change can improve conformance while changing how existing interactions behave.

49
00:02:58,845 --> 00:03:03,006
Internal DOM changes may be safe under a documented encapsulation policy,

50
00:03:03,006 --> 00:03:07,546
but tests, selectors, and custom styles can still depend on them in

51
00:03:07,546 --> 00:03:07,924
practice.

52
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:12,192
Behavior and presentation can break consumers · 2

53
00:03:13,318 --> 00:03:15,942
Build an inventory of supported customization points.

54
00:03:15,942 --> 00:03:21,191
If teams were instructed to use an internal class, it is not really internal.

55
00:03:21,191 --> 00:03:26,441
If the harness supplies the supported interaction API, update the harness with the component.

56
00:03:26,441 --> 00:03:30,190
Theme tokens need contrast and visual checks; a darker color

57
00:03:30,190 --> 00:03:33,565
is not automatically a breaking change or automatically harmless.

58
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:37,947
Behavior and presentation can break consumers · 3

59
00:03:39,072 --> 00:03:44,332
Raise a minimum Angular peer version only when dropping support is intentional and tested.

60
00:03:44,332 --> 00:03:48,842
A package that claims compatibility with a range should install and compile

61
00:03:48,842 --> 00:03:53,351
representative consumers across that range, including its forms and testing entry points.

62
00:03:53,351 --> 00:03:57,485
Passing on the newest framework alone does not validate the minimum.

63
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:01,113
Deprecation preserves behavior while changing guidance

64
00:04:02,238 --> 00:04:05,242
A deprecation introduces a supported replacement while keeping

65
00:04:05,242 --> 00:04:08,247
the old contract usable for an announced period.

66
00:04:08,247 --> 00:04:12,378
In the button rename, both inputs need an unset state so

67
00:04:12,378 --> 00:04:16,133
resolution can distinguish absence from an explicitly selected primary value.

68
00:04:16,133 --> 00:04:20,265
The new input wins, then the old input, then the default.

69
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:24,341
Deprecation preserves behavior while changing guidance · 2

70
00:04:25,466 --> 00:04:29,517
Warnings should be actionable and limited to actual deprecated usage in development.

71
00:04:29,517 --> 00:04:31,543
A computed derivation should stay pure.

72
00:04:31,543 --> 00:04:35,257
Put the diagnostic side effect in an appropriate effect and choose

73
00:04:35,257 --> 00:04:38,634
whether once means once per instance or once per application.

74
00:04:38,634 --> 00:04:39,647
Document that choice.

75
00:04:39,647 --> 00:04:42,686
Do not teach teams to ignore a flood of

76
00:04:42,686 --> 00:04:45,725
warnings from components that already use the new name.

77
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,253
Deprecation preserves behavior while changing guidance · 3

78
00:04:51,378 --> 00:04:53,823
Compatibility also includes direct component references.

79
00:04:53,823 --> 00:04:58,714
If callers read the old input getter, changing its default from primary

80
00:04:58,714 --> 00:05:03,197
to undefined is observable even when the rendered appearance is unchanged.

81
00:05:03,197 --> 00:05:06,050
State that limitation and inventory such uses.

82
00:05:06,050 --> 00:05:10,941
For strict compatibility, consider a distinct resolved property or a bridging API.

83
00:05:10,941 --> 00:05:15,424
A visual equivalence claim should not erase a public property change.

84
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:19,492
Migrations need more than a search and replace

85
00:05:20,617 --> 00:05:24,654
A template migration can rename a static kind attribute and a bound

86
00:05:24,654 --> 00:05:28,355
kind input when it knows the element is the library component.

87
00:05:28,355 --> 00:05:32,056
It should not rewrite every unrelated kind attribute in the repository.

88
00:05:32,056 --> 00:05:35,420
Parse templates and resolve symbols where the tooling supports it,

89
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:38,784
then report uncertain cases rather than applying a destructive guess.

90
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,863
Migrations need more than a search and replace · 2

91
00:05:44,988 --> 00:05:48,118
Programmatic setInput calls, host directive aliases, dynamic templates,

92
00:05:48,118 --> 00:05:50,856
and computed property names need separate analysis.

93
00:05:50,856 --> 00:05:54,378
A migration can handle statically resolvable string names and

94
00:05:54,378 --> 00:05:57,899
known imports, while emitting a report for dynamic uses.

95
00:05:57,899 --> 00:06:01,812
Make migrations repeatable and test both changed and unchanged files.

96
00:06:01,812 --> 00:06:05,725
The report should explain the manual correction and replacement API.

97
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:11,138
Migrations need more than a search and replace · 3

98
00:06:12,263 --> 00:06:17,204
Publish a changelog, replacement examples, editor deprecation hints, and a removal version.

99
00:06:17,204 --> 00:06:20,087
Gather data from repository scans, CI builds,

100
00:06:20,087 --> 00:06:23,381
and consumer owners before removing the old contract.

101
00:06:23,381 --> 00:06:29,146
Runtime diagnostics can help during development, but avoid secretly transmitting consumer code or telemetry.

102
00:06:29,146 --> 00:06:33,265
Migration evidence should be explicit and proportionate to the organization.

103
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,693
Package boundaries and peer dependencies

104
00:06:37,819 --> 00:06:41,864
Angular libraries should cooperate with the application's Angular installation.

105
00:06:41,864 --> 00:06:47,709
Declaring Angular framework packages as ordinary runtime dependencies can allow multiple incompatible copies.

106
00:06:47,709 --> 00:06:51,754
That can lead to injection context and identity problems,

107
00:06:51,754 --> 00:06:56,699
although a specific NG0203 error still needs diagnosis rather than a

108
00:06:56,699 --> 00:06:57,599
one-cause assumption.

109
00:06:57,599 --> 00:07:01,645
Peer dependencies express the versions the consumer must supply.

110
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:05,412
Package boundaries and peer dependencies · 2

111
00:07:06,537 --> 00:07:09,713
Use explicit exports to keep the public surface deliberate.

112
00:07:09,713 --> 00:07:13,595
A wildcard export can publish internal helpers and component rows accidentally.

113
00:07:13,595 --> 00:07:17,124
Separate entry points let consumers import the feature they need,

114
00:07:17,124 --> 00:07:22,065
and a testing entry point can expose harnesses without making them part of the

115
00:07:22,065 --> 00:07:23,124
main application API.

116
00:07:23,124 --> 00:07:28,065
However, an entry point alone does not make a dependency optional at installation time.

117
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:31,933
Package boundaries and peer dependencies · 3

118
00:07:33,058 --> 00:07:36,262
If a date picker depends on a date library,

119
00:07:36,262 --> 00:07:39,822
simply moving it to a secondary entry point may improve

120
00:07:39,822 --> 00:07:43,383
imports and bundles but still leave a required root dependency.

121
00:07:43,383 --> 00:07:46,587
Making an optional peer safe requires isolating references and

122
00:07:46,587 --> 00:07:49,792
documenting the feature's requirement, or using a separate package.

123
00:07:49,792 --> 00:07:52,996
Evaluate package manager installation, TypeScript resolution, and bundling separately.

124
00:07:52,996 --> 00:07:55,844
They are different phases with different failure modes.

125
00:07:57,220 --> 00:07:59,132
Release gates and support policy

126
00:08:00,257 --> 00:08:04,231
A release gate can compare public declarations, compile representative templates,

127
00:08:04,231 --> 00:08:07,807
run behavior and accessibility tests, inspect the packed artifact,

128
00:08:07,807 --> 00:08:09,397
and review theme snapshots.

129
00:08:09,397 --> 00:08:11,781
A generated API diff is useful,

130
00:08:11,781 --> 00:08:14,961
but it cannot understand every default or keyboard

131
00:08:14,961 --> 00:08:15,358
change.

132
00:08:15,358 --> 00:08:20,525
Combine mechanical checks with a release checklist that asks whether consumer behavior changed.

133
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:24,523
Release gates and support policy · 2

134
00:08:25,648 --> 00:08:30,895
Decide how many major lines to support according to staffing and consumer upgrade schedules.

135
00:08:30,895 --> 00:08:36,142
Support should state security patches, critical fixes, supported framework ranges, and the end date.

136
00:08:36,142 --> 00:08:39,890
A breaking security fix may need a new major and

137
00:08:39,890 --> 00:08:43,263
compatible backports or a documented mitigation for older lines.

138
00:08:43,263 --> 00:08:47,011
Communicate urgency and migration steps; do not silently redefine a

139
00:08:47,011 --> 00:08:50,385
minor release as breaking while claiming ordinary semantic versioning.

140
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:54,620
Release gates and support policy · 3

141
00:08:55,745 --> 00:09:00,239
Before publishing, install the packed package into a clean consumer project.

142
00:09:00,239 --> 00:09:04,733
Test each supported entry point without relying on monorepo path mappings.

143
00:09:04,733 --> 00:09:08,002
Verify peers, declarations, exports, assets, and dependency isolation.

144
00:09:08,002 --> 00:09:11,270
The consumer should receive the package you tested,

145
00:09:11,270 --> 00:09:15,356
not an internal build layout that accidentally makes missing files

146
00:09:15,356 --> 00:09:15,765
available.

147
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:19,040
VER-001 · Scenario

148
00:09:20,165 --> 00:09:23,910
We now review VER-001: Is this a breaking change?.

149
00:09:23,910 --> 00:09:28,905
Inspect the consumer contract and identify the mechanism behind each reported defect.

150
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:32,040
VER-001 · Scenario

151
00:09:33,166 --> 00:09:36,510
@acme/ui is at version 4.6.0 and follows semantic versioning.

152
00:09:36,510 --> 00:09:41,342
The release manager lists the changes planned for the next release of ui-select,

153
00:09:41,342 --> 00:09:46,545
and asks you to decide which ones force the next version to be 5.0.0.

154
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:51,889
VER-001 · Scenario · block 1 · page 1

155
00:09:53,014 --> 00:09:56,261
This is the original code, part 1 of 1.

156
00:09:56,261 --> 00:09:58,426
Focus on SelectOption, SelectComponent, options, size.

157
00:09:58,426 --> 00:10:02,035
Trace which element or value the consumer actually interacts with.

158
00:10:02,035 --> 00:10:06,005
We will compare this implementation with the correction after the question.

159
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:09,466
VER-001 · Scenario

160
00:10:10,591 --> 00:10:11,425
Planned changes:

161
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:15,550
VER-001 · Scenario · item 1

162
00:10:16,675 --> 00:10:19,425
Add an optional placeholder input with default ''.

163
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:23,569
VER-001 · Scenario · item 2

164
00:10:24,694 --> 00:10:26,425
Add a required label input.

165
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:30,540
VER-001 · Scenario · item 3

166
00:10:31,665 --> 00:10:34,405
Change the default size from 'medium' to 'small'.

167
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:38,607
VER-001 · Scenario · item 4

168
00:10:39,732 --> 00:10:43,265
Narrow size from string to 'small' | 'medium' | 'large'.

169
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:47,502
VER-001 · Scenario · item 5

170
00:10:48,627 --> 00:10:52,204
Widen the value input from SelectOption to SelectOption | null.

171
00:10:53,580 --> 00:10:56,468
VER-001 · Scenario · item 6

172
00:10:57,593 --> 00:11:01,564
Widen the selectionChange payload from SelectOption to SelectOption | null.

173
00:11:02,940 --> 00:11:05,677
VER-001 · Scenario · item 7

174
00:11:06,803 --> 00:11:13,304
Replace the internal <div> list with <ul>/<li> and rename internal CSS classes.

175
00:11:14,679 --> 00:11:18,396
VER-001 · Scenario · item 8

176
00:11:19,522 --> 00:11:21,845
Darken the semantic token --ui-color-action-primary.

177
00:11:23,220 --> 00:11:26,029
VER-001 · Scenario · item 9

178
00:11:27,154 --> 00:11:30,665
Make Space select the highlighted option (today only Enter does).

179
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:35,487
VER-001 · Scenario · item 10

180
00:11:36,613 --> 00:11:41,785
Stop exporting SelectOptionRowComponent, which was exported from public-api.ts by accident.

181
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:46,660
VER-001 · Scenario · item 11

182
00:11:47,785 --> 00:11:51,285
Switch the component to ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush.

183
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:56,718
VER-001 · Scenario · item 12

184
00:11:57,844 --> 00:12:03,425
Raise the @angular/core peer dependency from >=20 to >=21.

185
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:06,877
VER-001 · Interview question

186
00:12:08,002 --> 00:12:11,833
For each change, is it breaking, not breaking, or "it depends"?

187
00:12:11,833 --> 00:12:12,878
Justify each one.

188
00:12:12,878 --> 00:12:17,755
Then tell me how you would stop accidental breaking changes from reaching a release.

189
00:12:24,405 --> 00:12:26,083
VER-001 · Answer

190
00:12:27,208 --> 00:12:30,757
A working definition: a change is breaking if a consumer

191
00:12:30,757 --> 00:12:34,307
who used only the documented public API has to change

192
00:12:34,307 --> 00:12:37,856
their code, or sees behaviour they reasonably relied on change.

193
00:12:37,856 --> 00:12:42,116
That makes writing down what the public API is (exports, selectors, inputs,

194
00:12:42,116 --> 00:12:46,376
outputs, types, tokens, keyboard behaviour) the first job of a library team.

195
00:12:47,751 --> 00:12:51,382
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 1

196
00:12:52,508 --> 00:12:53,416
#: 1.

197
00:12:53,416 --> 00:12:56,140
Change: Optional input with a default.

198
00:12:56,140 --> 00:12:57,503
Verdict: Not breaking.

199
00:12:57,503 --> 00:13:01,136
Why: Existing templates compile and behave the same..

200
00:13:02,511 --> 00:13:05,779
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 2

201
00:13:06,904 --> 00:13:07,800
#: 2.

202
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:09,144
Change: Required input.

203
00:13:09,144 --> 00:13:10,040
Verdict: Breaking.

204
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:13,177
Why: Every existing <ui-select> fails to compile.

205
00:13:13,177 --> 00:13:17,210
Stage it: optional with a dev-mode warning in a

206
00:13:17,210 --> 00:13:20,796
minor, required in the next major (see A11Y-001)..

207
00:13:22,171 --> 00:13:25,350
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 3

208
00:13:26,475 --> 00:13:27,269
#: 3.

209
00:13:27,269 --> 00:13:28,859
Change: New default size.

210
00:13:28,859 --> 00:13:29,653
Verdict: Breaking.

211
00:13:29,653 --> 00:13:35,217
Why: No compile error, but every select that relied on the default changes size.

212
00:13:35,217 --> 00:13:38,396
Silent visual changes are the most expensive kind..

213
00:13:39,771 --> 00:13:43,080
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 4

214
00:13:44,206 --> 00:13:45,033
#: 4.

215
00:13:45,033 --> 00:13:47,515
Change: Narrow size to a union.

216
00:13:47,515 --> 00:13:48,343
Verdict: Breaking.

217
00:13:48,343 --> 00:13:51,652
Why: Call sites passing other strings stop compiling.

218
00:13:51,652 --> 00:13:55,376
They were probably bugs, but the upgrade still fails..

219
00:13:56,751 --> 00:13:59,723
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 5

220
00:14:00,848 --> 00:14:01,628
#: 5.

221
00:14:01,628 --> 00:14:03,579
Change: Widen an input type.

222
00:14:03,579 --> 00:14:05,140
Verdict: Usually not breaking.

223
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:09,042
Why: Callers write inputs; accepting more values breaks no caller.

224
00:14:09,042 --> 00:14:12,944
It is breaking for code that reads select.value() from a

225
00:14:12,944 --> 00:14:16,456
component reference, because the result can now be null..

226
00:14:17,831 --> 00:14:21,367
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 6

227
00:14:22,492 --> 00:14:23,376
#: 6.

228
00:14:23,376 --> 00:14:25,586
Change: Widen an output payload.

229
00:14:25,586 --> 00:14:26,470
Verdict: Breaking.

230
00:14:26,470 --> 00:14:28,238
Why: Consumers read outputs.

231
00:14:28,238 --> 00:14:31,775
A handler typed (option: SelectOption) now receives null,

232
00:14:31,775 --> 00:14:35,753
which strict templates reject, and untyped handlers crash at

233
00:14:35,753 --> 00:14:36,196
runtime..

234
00:14:37,571 --> 00:14:40,608
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 7

235
00:14:41,733 --> 00:14:42,574
#: 7.

236
00:14:42,574 --> 00:14:45,100
Change: Internal DOM and class names.

237
00:14:45,100 --> 00:14:48,467
Verdict: Not breaking, if the policy says so.

238
00:14:48,467 --> 00:14:52,256
Why: DOM structure and internal classes must be documented

239
00:14:52,256 --> 00:14:56,044
as private, and tests should use the library's harnesses.

240
00:14:56,044 --> 00:15:01,096
Still list it in the changelog: Hyrum's law says someone styled .ui-select__row..

241
00:15:02,471 --> 00:15:05,467
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 8

242
00:15:06,592 --> 00:15:07,380
#: 8.

243
00:15:07,380 --> 00:15:08,957
Change: Token value change.

244
00:15:08,957 --> 00:15:11,718
Verdict: Not breaking API; a design change.

245
00:15:11,718 --> 00:15:16,055
Why: Release it in a minor with release notes and screenshots.

246
00:15:16,055 --> 00:15:21,576
It must still meet contrast in every theme; screenshot tests in products will change..

247
00:15:22,951 --> 00:15:26,031
VER-001 · Answer · comparison 9

248
00:15:27,156 --> 00:15:27,926
#: 9.

249
00:15:27,926 --> 00:15:29,080
Change: Space selects.

250
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:31,776
Verdict: It depends; usually a minor fix.

251
