VER-001 · Follow-up 1

290
00:17:38,457 --> 00:17:40,890
A security fix requires a breaking change.

291
00:17:40,890 --> 00:17:45,063
How do you release it to teams who are two majors behind?

292
00:17:50,317 --> 00:17:53,653
Publish the fix according to the compatibility policy and urgency.

293
00:17:53,653 --> 00:17:55,989
If a compatible security backport is feasible,

294
00:17:55,989 --> 00:17:58,658
release it on supported older majors with tests.

295
00:17:58,658 --> 00:18:00,994
If the fix truly requires a break,

296
00:18:00,994 --> 00:18:04,330
publish the new major with a focused migration and explain

297
00:18:04,330 --> 00:18:07,667
the risk and any temporary mitigation for teams that cannot

298
00:18:07,667 --> 00:18:08,334
upgrade immediately.

299
00:18:08,334 --> 00:18:10,002
Coordinate disclosure and support windows.

300
00:18:10,002 --> 00:18:13,005
Do not conceal a breaking change inside a supposedly

301
00:18:13,005 --> 00:18:15,675
compatible patch merely because the reason is security.

302
00:18:17,050 --> 00:18:19,195
VER-001 · Follow-up 2

303
00:18:20,320 --> 00:18:25,530
How many major versions would you support at once, and what does "support" mean?

304
00:18:30,840 --> 00:18:34,414
Choose the number of supported major lines from team capacity,

305
00:18:34,414 --> 00:18:38,346
consumer release cadence, and risk, then publish exact dates and coverage.

306
00:18:38,346 --> 00:18:42,279
Support might include security patches and critical regressions without new features.

307
00:18:42,279 --> 00:18:46,211
State supported Angular ranges and what happens after end of support.

308
00:18:46,211 --> 00:18:49,786
One current line and one previous line can be practical

309
00:18:49,786 --> 00:18:53,360
for some organizations, but it is not a universal answer.

310
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:58,008
Make the commitment sustainable and measurable rather than promising every historical version indefinitely.

311
00:18:59,383 --> 00:19:01,478
VER-001 · Follow-up 3

312
00:19:02,603 --> 00:19:06,555
Would you ever ship a breaking change in a minor release?

313
00:19:06,555 --> 00:19:07,633
Under what policy?

314
00:19:13,015 --> 00:19:17,204
Under ordinary semantic versioning, a breaking change belongs in a major release.

315
00:19:17,204 --> 00:19:19,997
If the organization adopts an explicit exception policy,

316
00:19:19,997 --> 00:19:24,186
communicate it before consumers rely on minor compatibility, label the change prominently,

317
00:19:24,186 --> 00:19:26,281
and provide migration and risk guidance.

318
00:19:26,281 --> 00:19:30,121
Calling a break a bug fix does not make it compatible.

319
00:19:30,121 --> 00:19:33,263
Prefer a new major or a staged opt-in path.

320
00:19:33,263 --> 00:19:38,150
Any exception weakens the useful guarantee of minor upgrades, so explain the trade-off honestly.

